A Mathematical Theory of Multiplanetary Civilizational Viability

Constrained Flow, Dynamic Buffers, Quantitative Cascades, and the Topology of Distributed Survival

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 


Revision Note (v1.2 → v1.3)

This version incorporates eight structural corrections identified in review and checked against the viability theory, cascading failure, linear programming, and dynamic flow network literature.

1. Constraint (F4) deleted. The supply–demand balance Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}} + \sum_j r_{ji}^k - \sum_j r_{ij}^k \ge z_{ik} in v1.2 prevented consumption from drawing on stored buffer, contradicting the stated role of buffers. The buffer balance (F5) already enforces physical consistency, and (F4) is removed.
2. Buffer-violation slack introduced. The LP is reformulated with a slack variable \xi_{ik} \ge 0 that relaxes the buffer balance and is penalized in the objective. The LP is now always feasible, including when B_i^k < B_{i,\min}^k or when leakage and regeneration cost exceed production.
3. Cascade mechanisms unified. The intra-node cascade term \mathcal{D}_{k\ell} Q \phi in the production ODE of v1.2 is removed. All cross-function and cross-node degradation is handled by the quantitative cascade operator of Chapter 7. There is now exactly one cascade mechanism.
4. Regeneration costed. The regeneration term \mu_i (Q^{\mathrm{nom}} - Q)_+ now consumes buffer at rate c_{\mathrm{reg}} \mu_i (Q^{\mathrm{nom}} - Q)_+, where c_{\mathrm{reg}} \ge 0 is a dimensional constant. Regeneration competes with consumption for buffer. H5 is therefore a statement about a resourced process, not a free one.
5. Cascade convergence restated. Proposition 7.2 is reformulated as a statement about the failure set F \subseteq \mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}, not about Q itself. The map \Phi_q : F \mapsto F is monotone on a finite lattice, and Knaster–Tarski gives the least fixed point. The compounding decay of Q \cdot \eta is no longer conflated with convergence.
6. Spectral indicator corrected. The row-normalized coupling matrix \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}} has \lambda_{\max} = 1, so the v1.2 indicator \mu_{\mathrm{spec}} = 1 - \lambda_{\max} was identically zero. It is replaced by the subdominant-eigenvalue connectivity indicator

\rho_c = 1 - |\lambda_2(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}})|,

which measures mixing of the coupling and lies in [0,1].

7. Theorem 2.3′ scope clarified. The Lipschitz hypothesis on f is not satisfied by the discrete engine, because the LP argmax and the threshold cascade are discontinuous in the state. A remark states this explicitly and describes the regularized engine (entropic LP smoothing, sigmoid degradation) to which the theorem applies.
8. Notation disambiguated. Regeneration rate is \mu_i; disturbance law is \mu_w; connectivity indicator is \rho_c; signed distance is \delta_{\mathcal{V}}; margin in Theorem 2.3′ is \delta_K; transition sharpness is \chi(\mu); regeneration cost coefficient is c_{\mathrm{reg}}. The symbol \lambda is reserved for eigenvalues.

