Abstract

This monograph develops a mathematical framework for the viability of a civilization distributed across multiple astronomical nodes. We formulate multiplanetary viability as a problem in constrained stochastic control, network flow, and multilayer cascade dynamics. A civilization is represented as a temporal multilayer network of nodes, each carrying a vector-valued state describing production, human capital, resilience, autonomy, buffer stocks, and regeneration capacity across a set of critical functions. Inter-node links are dynamic objects characterized by capacity, latency, availability windows, reliability, and in-transit buffer.

The central object is the Multiplanetary Viability Kernel \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}, defined independently of any deterministic or robust counterpart. We derive the Planetary Independence Index PI_{\mathrm{dist}} as the output of a constrained linear program in flow rates with a full discrete buffer balance and buffer-violation slack. We introduce a quantitative cascade operator with monotone Lipschitz degradation functions, self-dependence excluded, whose convergence is stated as a fixed-point property on the failure set. The Cascade Margin is defined as a signed metric distance, distinguished from its empirical estimate. Regeneration enters the production dynamics and consumes buffer explicitly, so that the derivative \partial P_{\mathrm{survival}} / \partial \mu is a measurable quantity with a nontrivial cost structure.

We provide a full computational specification of a simulation engine and formulate a falsifiable experimental protocol centered on the quantity

\Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} = P_{\mathrm{survival}}(G_{\mathrm{distributed}}) - P_{\mathrm{survival}}(G_{\mathrm{Earth\text{-}dependent}}).

The framework is designed to allow — and to seek — its own refutation.

