Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 Framing

The transition from a single-planet civilization to a multiplanetary one is conventionally framed as a problem of propulsion, life support, or colonization. This framing is operationally useful but theoretically impoverished: it treats destinations as objects of study rather than the dynamical structure of the civilization itself.

We reframe multiplanetary viability as a problem in viability theory over a multilayer temporal network. A multiplanetary civilization is not defined by the presence of humans beyond Earth; it is defined by the existence of a viable region of state space from which essential functions can be preserved under admissible perturbations and partial node loss.

1.2 Contributions

1. A vector-valued formalism for state, autonomy, human capital, and regeneration capacity.
2. A formulation of the stochastic viability kernel \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} via dynamic programming, kept strictly distinct from the robust kernel \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} and the deterministic kernel \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T}, with a finite-horizon margin theorem (Theorem 2.3′) replacing the invalid infinite-horizon inclusion of v1.0.
3. A constrained linear program in flow rates r_{ij}^k, with a full discrete buffer balance and buffer-violation slack, from which PI_{\mathrm{dist}} is derived as an output.
4. A buffer ODE coupled to flow rates and to regeneration cost, capturing intermittent interplanetary links.
5. A quantitative cascade operator with monotone Lipschitz degradation functions g_{k\ell}, self-dependence excluded, whose convergence is stated as a fixed-point property on the failure set.
6. A signed Cascade Margin sCM defined exactly, and an empirical estimate \widehat{CM} obtained by a level-set surrogate with matching sign convention.
7. Explicit regeneration dynamics in the production rate, with a buffer cost, making \partial P_{\mathrm{survival}} / \partial \mu a measurable derivative of a resourced process.
8. A corrected connectivity indicator \rho_c based on the subdominant eigenvalue of the row-normalized coupling matrix.
9. A computational engine with algorithms in pseudocode.
10. A falsifiable experimental protocol with \Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} as its central observable.

1.3 Prior Mathematical Foundations

The framework draws on four bodies of mathematics:

· Viability theory (Aubin 1991; Aubin & Saint-Pierre 2011; Saint-Pierre): tangential conditions, viability kernels, signed distance regularization.
· Stochastic viability (Buckdahn et al. 2000; Cardaliaguet 2007): HJB equations, value functions, level-set characterization of viability kernels via smallest viscosity supersolutions.
· Multilayer networks and cascades (Buldyrev et al. 2010; Gao, Barzel, Barabási 2016): cascading failures, monotone cascade maps, fixed-point convergence via Knaster–Tarski.
· Constrained and dynamic flow (Bertsimas & Tsitsiklis 1997; dynamic flow network literature): LP duality, max-flow min-cut, buffer dynamics under conservation of mass.

The synthesis, not any single tool, is novel.

