Chapter 2 — Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Controlled Dynamical Systems

Let \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n be the state space, \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m the control space, \mathcal{W} the disturbance space. A controlled system is

\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t), u(t), w(t)), \quad x(0) = x_0

with u(\cdot) \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}, w(\cdot) \in \mathcal{W}.

Definition 2.1. A closed set \mathcal{K} \subseteq \mathcal{X} is a constraint set. A trajectory x(\cdot) is viable in \mathcal{K} if x(t) \in \mathcal{K} for all t \ge 0.

Definition 2.2 (Exit time).

\tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0, u, w) = \inf \{ t \ge 0 : x(t; x_0, u, w) \notin \mathcal{K} \}

with \tau_{\mathcal{K}} = +\infty if x(t) \in \mathcal{K} for all t.

2.2 Deterministic Viability Kernel

Definition 2.3.

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} = \left\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K} : \exists u(\cdot),\ x(t; x_0, u, w = 0) \in \mathcal{K} \ \forall t \in [0,T] \right\}.

Theorem 2.1 (Aubin). Under standard regularity, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},\infty} is the largest closed subset \mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathcal{K} such that

\forall x \in \mathcal{D},\ \exists u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} : f(x,u,0) \in T_{\mathcal{D}}(x)

where T_{\mathcal{D}}(x) is the contingent cone.

2.3 Stochastic Viability Kernel

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t \ge 0}, \mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space and w(\cdot) an (\mathcal{F}_t)-adapted disturbance with law \mu_w.

Definition 2.4.

\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \left\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K} : \sup_{u(\cdot)} \mathbb{P}\left[ \tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0,u,w) > T \right] \ge \beta \right\}.

Definition 2.5 (Value function).

V(t,x) = \sup_{u(\cdot)} \mathbb{P}\left[ x(s) \in \mathcal{K},\ \forall s \in [t,T] \mid x(t) = x \right].

Theorem 2.2 (HJB characterization). Under standard assumptions, V is the smallest nonnegative lower-semicontinuous viscosity supersolution of

-\partial_t V - \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \mathcal{L}^u V = 0

with boundary data

V(T, x) = 1_{\mathcal{K}}(x), \qquad V(t, x) = 0 \ \text{for } x \notin \mathcal{K},

and \mathcal{L}^u the generator of the controlled diffusion. The kernel is recovered as the superlevel set

\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \{ x : V(0,x) \ge \beta \}.

Remark 2.1. For the exit-time problem considered here, the correct characterization is the smallest nonnegative semicontinuous viscosity supersolution. The formulation as a viscosity solution, used in v1.0, is weaker and does not uniquely determine V. The supersolution formulation is the one that yields the level-set representation of \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}.

2.4 Robust Viability Kernel

Definition 2.6.

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} = \left\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K} : \exists u(\cdot),\ \forall w(\cdot) \in \mathcal{W},\ x(t;x_0,u,w) \in \mathcal{K}\ \forall t \ge 0 \right\}.

Remark 2.2 (open-loop vs. closed-loop). Definition 2.6 uses open-loop control: the same u(\cdot) must work for every w(\cdot) \in \mathcal{W}, with no feedback on the realized disturbance. A closed-loop robust kernel \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}^{\mathrm{cl}} permits u(t) to depend on the observed state. In general \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}^{\mathrm{cl}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}. The open-loop formulation is used here because it is conservative (larger kernel) and because it is the standard in the viability literature. Closed-loop extensions are left to future work.

2.5 Non-Inclusions and Conditional Relationships

Remark 2.3 (no universal hierarchy). In general,

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} \not\subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} \not\subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}.

Relations among the three require explicit hypotheses.

Theorem 2.3′ (finite-horizon margin theorem). Assume:

· f : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{W} \to \mathbb{R}^n is Lipschitz in x with constant L_x and in w with constant L_w;
· \mathcal{W} is bounded with \|w\|_\infty \le W_{\max};
· T < \infty;
· x_0 \in \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} with a deterministic viable trajectory x^*(\cdot) satisfying the uniform margin condition

\delta_K := \inf_{t \in [0,T]} \operatorname{dist}(x^*(t), \partial\mathcal{K}) > 0.

Then x_0 \in \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} for every

\beta \le \mu_w\!\left( \left\{ w : \|w\|_\infty < \frac{\delta_K L_x}{L_w (e^{L_x T} - 1)} \right\} \right).

Proof. By Grönwall's inequality applied to \dot{x} - \dot{x}^* = f(x,u^*,w) - f(x^*,u^*,0), the deviation obeys

\|x(t) - x^*(t)\| \le \frac{L_w \|w\|_\infty}{L_x}\big(e^{L_x t} - 1\big) \le \frac{L_w \varepsilon}{L_x}\big(e^{L_x T} - 1\big)

whenever \|w\|_\infty \le \varepsilon. Choose

\varepsilon_0 = \frac{\delta_K L_x}{L_w (e^{L_x T} - 1)}.

For \|w\|_\infty < \varepsilon_0, the trajectory remains within distance \delta_K of x^*, hence in the interior of \mathcal{K} on [0,T]. The set \{\|w\|_\infty < \varepsilon_0\} has \mu_w-measure at least \beta_0 := \mu_w(\{\|w\|_\infty < \varepsilon_0\}), which is positive provided w \equiv 0 lies in the support of \mu_w. Thus x_0 \in \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} for all \beta \le \beta_0. \square

Remark 2.4. The infinite-horizon inclusion \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},\infty} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty} is not claimed. A deterministic trajectory may approach \partial\mathcal{K} asymptotically, in which case arbitrarily small disturbances can eventually push it out and the probability of survival for all time may be zero. For T = \infty the correct object is the robust kernel.

Remark 2.5 (scope). Theorem 2.3′ requires f Lipschitz in (x,w). The discrete engine of Chapter 9 does not satisfy this: the flow LP argmax and the threshold cascade are discontinuous in the state. The theorem therefore applies to the continuous-time ODE model of Chapters 3 and 6, not directly to the discrete simulation. The regularized engine described in Remark 9.1 (entropic LP smoothing and sigmoid degradation functions) restores Lipschitz continuity and brings the engine within the scope of the theorem, at the cost of a small approximation error controlled by the regularization parameters.

Theorem 2.4 (stochastic vs. robust). Assume \mathcal{W} is the support of \mu_w, \mathcal{W} is compact, and V is continuous in x uniformly in t. Then

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty} \quad \text{for all } \beta \in [0,1].

Proof. If x_0 \in \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}, there exists u^* such that for all w \in \mathcal{W}, \tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0, u^*, w) = \infty. Since \mathcal{W} = \mathrm{supp}(\mu_w), the event \{\tau_{\mathcal{K}} = \infty\} has probability 1 under \mu_w. Hence \mathbb{P}[\tau_{\mathcal{K}} > T] = 1 \ge \beta. \square

Corollary. Under the hypotheses of Theorem 2.4, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} \subseteq \bigcap_{\beta < 1} \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty}. Equality is not claimed in general.

2.6 Multilayer Networks and Cascades

Definition 2.7 (multilayer network). A tuple \mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{intra}}, \mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{inter}}) with \mathcal{N} the node set, \mathcal{F} the layer (function) set, and \mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{intra}}, \mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{inter}} the within-layer and cross-layer edge sets.

Definition 2.8 (cascade map). A map \Phi : \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}) \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}) is a cascade map if it is monotone with respect to inclusion and \Phi(\emptyset) = \emptyset.

Proposition 2.5 (Knaster–Tarski / Kleene). If \Phi is monotone and \mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F} is finite, then \Phi has a smallest fixed point containing any given initial failure set F_0, reached in at most |\mathcal{N}| \cdot |\mathcal{F}| iterations of \Phi applied to F_0.

Proof. The sequence F_0 \subseteq \Phi(F_0) \subseteq \Phi^2(F_0) \subseteq \cdots is increasing in the finite lattice \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}), hence stabilizes. The limit is a fixed point, and by monotonicity it is the smallest one containing F_0. \square

