Chapter 3 — Civilizational State Space

3.1 Nodes and Functions

Definition 3.1. \mathcal{N} = \{E, L, O, M, A\} (Earth, Moon, Orbital, Mars, Asteroid).

Definition 3.2. \mathcal{F} = \{\mathrm{En}, \mathrm{In}, \mathrm{Med}, \mathrm{Hab}\}.

3.2 Node State

Definition 3.3.

C_i(t) = \left( Q_i^{\mathrm{prod}}(t),\ H_i(t),\ R_i(t),\ A_i(t),\ B_i(t),\ \mu_i \right)

with:

· Q_i^{\mathrm{prod}} \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|\mathcal{F}|} (production rates, units/time);
· H_i = (P_i, N_i, S_i) \in \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathbb{R}_+ \times [0,1] (effective population, neural capital, social cohesion);
· R_i \in [0,1] (resilience index);
· A_i \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|\mathcal{F}|} (autonomy vector);
· B_i \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|\mathcal{F}|} (buffer stocks, units);
· \mu_i \in [0,1] (regeneration rate, 1/\mathrm{time}).

Definition 3.4 (autonomy ratio).

a_i^k(t) = \frac{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{loc}}(t)}{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{crit}}(t)}.

3.3 Constraint Set

Definition 3.5.

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} = \left\{ C \in \mathcal{X} : \text{(i)–(v) hold} \right\}

(i) Q_k^{\mathrm{global}}(C) \ge Q_k^{\min} for all k \in \mathcal{F}.

(ii) P_i \ge P_{\min}(\theta) for all i.

(iii) \mathrm{Cover}(H_i, \mathcal{S}) \ge \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}} for all i.

(iv) B_i^k \ge B_{i,\min}^k > 0 for all i, k.

(v) Cumulative unmet-demand constraint. For each i, k and each window [t_1, t_2],

\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \big(d_{ik}(s) - z_{ik}(s)\big)_+ \, ds \le U_{ik}^{\max}.

If the deficit exceeds U_{ik}^{\max}, the state leaves \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} or a cascade is triggered, per Chapter 7.

Remark 3.1. P_{\min}(\theta) is parameterized. Under effective-population theory (N_e \ge 10^3) with ratio N_e / P \in [0.1, 0.2], one obtains P_{\min} \in [5 \times 10^3, 10^4] as a baseline. This is one point in parameter space, not a universal constant.

3.4 Regeneration Dynamics

Definition 3.6 (regeneration capacity). Each node i carries a scalar \mu_i \in [0,1], interpreted as the rate at which lost production capacity is restored toward nominal, absent further damage.

Definition 3.7 (production dynamics with regeneration, no cascade).

\boxed{
\dot{Q}_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) = \mu_i \big(Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}} - Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t)\big)_+ - \gamma_\ell\, Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t)
}

where \gamma_\ell \ge 0 is a natural decay rate of function \ell.

Remark 3.2. In v1.2, Definition 3.7 included an intra-node cascade term \mathcal{D}_{k\ell} Q \phi. That term is removed. All cross-function and cross-node degradation is handled exclusively by the cascade operator of Chapter 7. There is now exactly one cascade mechanism. Definition 3.7 governs only regeneration and natural decay.

Remark 3.3 (resource cost of regeneration). The regeneration term is not free. It consumes buffer at rate

R_{i\ell}(t) := c_{\mathrm{reg}}\, \mu_i \big(Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}} - Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t)\big)_+

where c_{\mathrm{reg}} \ge 0 is a dimensional constant (units of time) converting regeneration rate to buffer cost. This term appears in the buffer ODE of Chapter 6 and in the buffer balance of the LP in Chapter 5. Regeneration therefore competes with consumption for buffer, and the "phase transition" of H5 is a statement about a resourced process.

