Chapter 4 — Viability Kernels: Three Independent Notions

Definition 4.1 (deterministic kernel).

\boxed{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} = \left\{ C_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} : \exists u(\cdot),\ C(t; C_0, u, w \equiv 0) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}},\ \forall t \in [0,T] \right\}.}

Definition 4.2 (stochastic kernel).

\boxed{\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \left\{ C_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} : \sup_{u(\cdot)} \mathbb{P}\left[ \tau_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}}(C_0, u, w) > T \right] \ge \beta \right\}.}

Definition 4.3 (robust kernel).

\boxed{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} = \left\{ C_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} : \exists u(\cdot),\ \forall w(\cdot) \in \mathcal{W},\ C(t; C_0, u, w) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}},\ \forall t \ge 0 \right\}.}

Proposition 4.4. Under the hypotheses of Theorem 2.3′, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} for \beta sufficiently small relative to the \mu_w-measure of the margin ball.

Proposition 4.5. Under the hypotheses of Theorem 2.4, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty} for every \beta \in [0,1].

Remark 4.1. No other general inclusion is asserted. In particular, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det}} and \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} need not be nested.

Definition 4.6 (viability boundary).

\partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \overline{\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}} \setminus \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}).

Definition 4.7 (signed distance to the kernel boundary).

\delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C) =
\begin{cases}
+d(C, \partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}), & C \in \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}, \\[1mm]
-d(C, \partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}), & C \notin \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}.
\end{cases}

The signed distance is locally Lipschitz off \partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} and is the natural regularization used in viability theory to characterize the kernel from its boundary.

The central theoretical objective is to characterize \partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} as a function of network topology, buffer capacities, flow rates, regeneration, and cascade coupling.

