Chapter 5 — Temporal Network and Constrained Flow

5.1 Temporal Graph

Definition 5.1.

G(t) = (\mathcal{N}, E(t)), \quad E(t) = \{ L_{ij}^k(t) : i,j \in \mathcal{N},\ k \in \mathcal{F} \}.

5.2 Link Structure

Definition 5.2.

L_{ij}^k(t) = \left( \Gamma_{ij}^k(t),\ \tau_{ij}(t),\ W_{ij}(t),\ p_{ij}^k,\ B_{ij}^k(t) \right)

with:

· \Gamma_{ij}^k(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+: capacity (units/time);
· \tau_{ij}(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+: latency (time);
· W_{ij}(t) \in \{0,1\}: availability;
· p_{ij}^k \in [0,1]: reliability;
· B_{ij}^k(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+: in-transit buffer.

Remark 5.1. The symbol C is reserved for the civilizational state. Link capacity is written \Gamma_{ij}^k(t). The symbol \mu is reserved for regeneration rate \mu_i and for the disturbance law \mu_w; the subscript distinguishes them.

5.3 Flow Rates

Definition 5.3. Let r_{ij}^k(t) denote the rate of flow of function k from i to j (units/time), r_{ij}^k \ge 0.

5.4 Maximum Feasible Delivered Rate with Full Buffer Balance and Slack

Definition 5.4 (feasible delivered rate). For surviving subset S \subseteq \mathcal{N}, horizon step \Delta t > 0, function k, penalty M > 0, and regeneration cost

R_{ik}(t) := c_{\mathrm{reg}}\, \mu_i \big(Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}} - Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t)\big)_+,

define

\boxed{D_k(S,t) = \max_{r, z, \xi} \left[ \Delta t \sum_{i \in S} z_{ik} - M \sum_{i \in S} \xi_{ik} \right]}

subject to

(F1) Demand bounds:

0 \le z_{ik} \le d_{ik}, \quad \forall i \in S.

(F2) Capacity bounds:

0 \le r_{ij}^k(t) \le \Gamma_{ij}^k(t), \quad \forall (i,j) \in S^2.

(F3) Availability and latency:

r_{ij}^k(t) = 0 \quad \text{if } W_{ij}(t) = 0 \text{ or } \tau_{ij}(t) > \tau_k^{\max}.

(F4) — deleted. See Remark 5.3.

(F5) Full discrete buffer balance with slack:

\boxed{
B_i^k(t) + \Delta t \Big( Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) + \sum_{j \in S} r_{ji}^k(t) - \sum_{j \in S} r_{ij}^k(t) - z_{ik} - \beta_k B_i^k(t) - R_{ik}(t) \Big) + \xi_{ik} \ge B_{i,\min}^k
}

for all i \in S.

(F6) Slack nonnegativity:

\xi_{ik} \ge 0, \quad \forall i \in S.

Remark 5.2. The LP is always feasible: r \equiv 0, z \equiv 0, and \xi_{ik} chosen large enough satisfy (F1)–(F3), (F5), (F6) regardless of the state. When the state is inside \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}, the optimum satisfies \xi^* = 0 for all i provided M is large enough. When the state is outside \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}, the minimal \sum_i \xi_{ik}^* measures the aggregate buffer violation.

Remark 5.3 (why (F4) is deleted). In v1.2, constraint (F4) required Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}} + \sum_j r_{ji}^k - \sum_j r_{ij}^k \ge z_{ik}, so delivered consumption could not exceed net supply and thus could never draw on stored buffer. This contradicted the stated role of buffers in absorbing blackouts (Chapter 6) and under-credited stockpiling in H4. The buffer balance (F5) already enforces physical consistency: if net supply falls short of z_{ik}, the buffer depletes, and the constraint B_i^k(t+\Delta t) \ge B_{i,\min}^k - \xi_{ik} rations z_{ik} when the buffer approaches its minimum. Deleting (F4) restores the intended role of buffers.

Remark 5.4. The regeneration cost R_{ik}(t) is a constant in the LP, because it depends only on Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t), \mu_i, Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}}, and c_{\mathrm{reg}}, all of which are fixed at time t. No nonlinearity is introduced by including it in (F5).

Proposition 5.5 (bounds). If the state is inside \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}, the LP optimum satisfies

0 \le D_k(S,t) \le \Delta t \cdot \sum_{i \in S} d_{ik}

and \xi_{ik}^* = 0 for all i \in S when M exceeds M_0 := \Delta t \sum_{i \in S} d_{ik}.

Proof. Upper bound from z_{ik} \le d_{ik}. For \xi^* = 0: the state C(t) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} implies B_i^k(t) \ge B_{i,\min}^k. The trivial allocation r = 0, z = 0, \xi = 0 satisfies (F5) provided

B_i^k(t) + \Delta t\big(Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) - \beta_k B_i^k(t) - R_{ik}(t)\big) \ge B_{i,\min}^k.

If this holds for all i, \xi^* = 0. If it fails for some i, then \xi_{ik}^* > 0 is required, and the penalty M\xi_{ik} ensures that the LP chooses the smallest such \xi. Taking M larger than the marginal value of z guarantees that \xi is minimized first. \square

5.5 Distributed Planetary Independence Index

Definition 5.6.

\boxed{PI_{\mathrm{dist}}(S,t) = \min_{k \in \mathcal{F}^+(S)} \frac{D_k(S,t)}{\Delta t \cdot \sum_{i \in S} d_{ik}}}

where \mathcal{F}^+(S) = \{k \in \mathcal{F} : \sum_{i \in S} d_{ik} > 0\}.

If \mathcal{F}^+(S) = \emptyset, set PI_{\mathrm{dist}}(S,t) = 1 by convention (no demand, no independence constraint).

Proposition 5.7. 0 \le PI_{\mathrm{dist}}(S,t) \le 1.

Proposition 5.8.

\boxed{PI_{\mathrm{dist}}(S,t) = 1 \iff D_k(S,t) = \Delta t \cdot \sum_{i \in S} d_{ik}\ \text{for all } k \in \mathcal{F}^+(S).}

Proof. (\Leftarrow) Immediate. (\Rightarrow) Suppose PI_{\mathrm{dist}} = 1. Then each ratio D_k / (\Delta t \sum_i d_{ik}) \ge 1. By Proposition 5.5, D_k \le \Delta t \sum_i d_{ik}, hence each ratio equals 1. \square

Remark 5.5. The criterion is PI_{\mathrm{dist}} = 1, not PI_{\mathrm{dist}} \ge 1. The boundedness of PI_{\mathrm{dist}} is a substantive property of the LP, and the equality formulation is the correct one.

