Chapter 7 — Quantitative Cascades

7.1 Dependency Structure

Definition 7.1. Intra-node dependency matrix \mathcal{D} \in [0,1]^{|\mathcal{F}| \times |\mathcal{F}|}, with \mathcal{D}_{k\ell} = maximum degradation of function \ell caused by total loss of function k. Diagonal entries \mathcal{D}_{kk} are excluded from cascade products (self-dependence).

Definition 7.2. Inter-node dependency matrix \mathcal{C} \in [0,1]^{|\mathcal{N}| \times |\mathcal{N}|}, with \mathcal{C}_{ji} = maximum degradation of node i caused by total loss of node j. Diagonal entries \mathcal{C}_{ii} are excluded.

7.2 Degradation Functions

Definition 7.3. A degradation function is a nonincreasing Lipschitz map g : [0,1] \to [0,1] with g(0) = 1 (no failure input, full capacity) and g(1) = 0 (total failure input, zero capacity).

Assumption 7.1 (regularity). The degradation functions g_{k\ell} and \tilde{g}_{ji} are nonincreasing and Lipschitz. Monotonicity is required for the cascade map to be monotone; Lipschitz continuity is required for the fixed-point iteration to be well posed.

7.3 Quantitative Cascade Update

Definition 7.4.

\boxed{
Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t + \Delta t) =
\Big[ Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) + \Delta t\big( \mu_i (Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}} - Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t))_+ - \gamma_\ell Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) \big) \Big]
\cdot
\eta_{i\ell}(t)
}

where

\eta_{i\ell}(t) =
\prod_{k \ne \ell} g_{k\ell}\!\left( 1 - \frac{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t)}{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}}} \right)
\cdot
\prod_{j \ne i} \tilde{g}_{ji}\!\left( 1 - \frac{Q_{j}^{\mathrm{agg}}(t)}{Q_{j}^{\mathrm{agg},\mathrm{nom}}} \right).

Remark 7.1. The bracket contains only regeneration and natural decay, per Definition 3.7. The factor \eta_{i\ell}(t) \in [0,1] contains the entire cascade effect. The two mechanisms are applied sequentially within a single time step, not double-counted. The exclusion k \ne \ell and j \ne i prevents a node's own state from appearing in its own degradation factor.

7.4 Binary Cascade as a Limit

Definition 7.5 (binary operator).

\Phi(F) = F \cup \left\{ (i,\ell) : \exists (j,k) \in F,\ (k,\ell) \ne (j,i),\ \text{with } \mathcal{D}_{k\ell} > \theta_D \text{ or } \mathcal{C}_{ji} > \theta_C \right\}.

Proposition 7.1. If g_{k\ell} and \tilde{g}_{ji} are step functions g(x) = 1_{x < \theta}, then the quantitative update of Definition 7.4 reduces to the binary operator \Phi on the set of nodes/functions whose capacity falls below threshold, with self-terms excluded.

Proof. The product of step functions is 1 unless at least one input exceeds threshold, in which case the product is 0 and the capacity collapses. Identifying collapsed capacities with the failure set F, and excluding self-terms in both formulations, recovers \Phi. \square

7.5 Convergence of the Quantitative Cascade

Proposition 7.2 (fixed-point convergence on the failure set). Let g_{k\ell}, \tilde{g}_{ji} be nonincreasing Lipschitz, and let \theta_q \in (0,1) be a failure threshold. Define the failure-set map \Phi_q : \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}) \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}) by

(i,\ell) \in \Phi_q(F) \iff Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t + \Delta t; F) < \theta_q \, Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}}

where Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t+\Delta t; F) is computed from Definition 7.4 with the failure set F determining which degradation factors are active. Then \Phi_q is monotone with respect to inclusion, \Phi_q(\emptyset) = \emptyset, and for any initial failure set F_0, the sequence F^{(n+1)} = \Phi_q(F^{(n)}) stabilizes in at most |\mathcal{N}| \cdot |\mathcal{F}| iterations at the smallest fixed point containing F_0.

Proof. Because each g_{k\ell} is nonincreasing in its argument and the argument 1 - Q_{ik}/Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}} is nondecreasing in the failure set F, the product \eta_{i\ell} is nonincreasing in F. Hence Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t+\Delta t; F) is nonincreasing in F, and the set of (i,\ell) whose capacity falls below threshold is nondecreasing in F. Thus \Phi_q is monotone. With \Phi_q(\emptyset) = \emptyset and the lattice \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N} \times \mathcal{F}) finite, the ascending Kleene chain stabilizes, and Proposition 2.5 gives the bound. \square

Remark 7.2. The object that converges is the failure set F, not Q itself. Under the update Q \mapsto Q \cdot \eta with \eta \in [0,1], Q decays geometrically; the nontrivial equilibrium arises only when regeneration balances cascade losses. Formulating the convergence statement on F avoids the confusion of v1.2, where convergence of Q was claimed but not delivered.

7.6 Connectivity Indicator

Definition 7.6 (connectivity indicator).

\boxed{\rho_c = 1 - |\lambda_2(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}})|}

where \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}} is the row-normalized coupling matrix (diagonal excluded, rows renormalized to sum to 1), and \lambda_2(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}}) is its subdominant eigenvalue.

Remark 7.3. The v1.2 indicator \mu_{\mathrm{spec}} = 1 - \lambda_{\max}(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}}) was identically zero, because a row-normalized nonnegative matrix has \lambda_{\max} = 1 by the Perron–Frobenius theorem. The corrected indicator \rho_c \in [0,1] measures the mixing rate of the coupling: \rho_c = 1 when |\lambda_2| = 0 (perfect mixing, no clustering), and \rho_c = 0 when |\lambda_2| = 1 (decoupled or clustered). It is an observable indicator, not a viability criterion. Its predictive value is tested empirically in Chapter 10.

---

Chapter 8 — Cascade Margin

8.1 Exact Definition

Definition 8.1 (signed Cascade Margin). Let d(\cdot, \cdot) be a metric on \mathcal{X} and \delta_{\mathcal{V}} the signed distance of Definition 4.7.

\boxed{sCM(C) = \delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C).}

Under a realized disturbance \omega \in \mathcal{W},

sCM_\omega(C) = \delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C_\omega).

Definition 8.2 (robust margin).

CM_{\mathrm{rob}}(C) = \inf_{\omega \in \mathcal{W}} sCM_\omega(C).

The system is robust at C if CM_{\mathrm{rob}}(C) > 0. The system is non-viable at C if CM_{\mathrm{rob}}(C) < 0. The system is on the viability boundary at C if CM_{\mathrm{rob}}(C) = 0.

Remark 8.1. The signed convention removes the ambiguity of v1.0 and v1.1: a point outside \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} has negative margin, a point inside has positive margin, and a point on \partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} has zero margin.

8.2 Computational Reality

Remark 8.2. The exact \partial \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} is generally not available. Computing it is a problem as hard as computing \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} itself.

We therefore introduce an empirical surrogate.

Definition 8.3 (surrogate).

\widehat{V}(C) = \widehat{P}_{\mathrm{survival}}(C) - \beta

where \widehat{P}_{\mathrm{survival}} is a Monte Carlo estimate.

Definition 8.4 (empirical margin).

\boxed{\widehat{CM}(C) = \frac{\widehat{V}(C)}{\|\nabla \widehat{V}(C)\|}}

using the first-order approximation to the signed distance to the level set \widehat{V} = 0.

Remark 8.3. The sign of \widehat{CM} matches the sign of sCM: positive inside \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}, negative outside, zero on the boundary. The distinction between sCM and \widehat{CM} is not cosmetic. sCM is a geometric object; \widehat{CM} is a computable estimate whose accuracy depends on the sample size, the smoothness of \widehat{V}, and the richness of the surrogate class.

