Chapter 10 — Experimental Design and Falsifiable Hypotheses

10.1 Hypotheses

H1 (redundancy). Increasing the number of partially independent nodes increases P_{\mathrm{survival}} even when no node is individually autonomous.

H2 (bottleneck). Viability is disproportionately constrained by low-capacity critical functions.

H3 (connectivity). Connectivity increases short-term resilience but excessive dependence on few links creates systemic vulnerability.

H4 (regeneration). A node capable of reproducing its own critical infrastructure has qualitatively greater long-term value than a node that merely stores resources, even after accounting for the buffer cost of regeneration.

H5 (phase transition). There exists a parameter regime in which increasing the local regeneration rate \mu produces a nonlinear transition in P_{\mathrm{survival}}.

10.2 Central Measurable Quantity

Definition 10.1 (Multiplanetary Delta).

\boxed{\Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} = P_{\mathrm{survival}}(G_{\mathrm{distributed}}) - P_{\mathrm{survival}}(G_{\mathrm{Earth\text{-}dependent}}).}

Definition 10.2 (transition sharpness).

\chi(\mu) = \frac{\partial P_{\mathrm{survival}}}{\partial \mu}.

H5 is supported if \chi exhibits a sharp and reproducible peak over a narrow interval [\mu_c - \epsilon, \mu_c + \epsilon]. H5 is refuted if \chi is uniformly smooth.

Remark 10.1 (null model for H5). Because regeneration now has a buffer cost c_{\mathrm{reg}} \mu (Q^{\mathrm{nom}} - Q)_+, a linear threshold effect near \mu \approx \gamma_\ell is already predicted by the single-node production ODE. H5 must therefore be tested against a null model: P_{\mathrm{survival}} as a function of \mu in a single-node, no-cascade system with the same regeneration cost. A phase transition is genuine only if the network-level transition is sharper than the single-node baseline, or if it appears at a \mu_c that depends on topology in a way the single-node model cannot reproduce.

10.3 Experiment 0 — Control

5 nodes × 4 functions. Topologies T_1, \dots, T_4. Scenarios S_1, \dots, S_4. Observe

\left( P_{\mathrm{collapse}},\ PI_{\mathrm{dist}},\ T_{\mathrm{rec}},\ \widehat{CM},\ \rho_c \right).

10.4 Experiment 1 — Sweep

Grid over (\rho, \tau_{\mathrm{norm}}, \sigma, \mu):

· \rho: redundancy,
· \tau_{\mathrm{norm}}: normalized latency,
· \sigma: inter-node dependency strength,
· \mu: regeneration rate.

Estimate P_{\mathrm{survival}} and \Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} on each grid point.

10.5 Tests of H5

Test A. Fix (\rho, \tau_{\mathrm{norm}}, \sigma). Compute \chi(\mu). Measure the width of the transition.

Test B. Repeat for several (\rho, \tau_{\mathrm{norm}}, \sigma). Test reproducibility.

Test C. Compute \max_\mu |\chi(\mu)| versus the total range P_{\mathrm{survival}} spans. A small span indicates a diffuse transition.

Test D (null-model comparison). Compare \chi(\mu) for the network to \chi_{\mathrm{single}}(\mu) for the single-node baseline with the same regeneration cost. H5 is supported only if the network transition is sharper than the single-node transition at matched \gamma_\ell and c_{\mathrm{reg}}.

10.6 Indicator Predictive Power

For each X \in \{PI_{\mathrm{dist}}, \widehat{CM}, \rho_c, T_{\mathrm{rec}}\}, fit a predictor of P_{\mathrm{collapse}} from X and compare cross-validated error. The framework commits to whichever indicator performs best, without presupposing the answer.

10.7 Falsifiability

The framework is falsifiable in at least five ways:

1. If P_{\mathrm{survival}} \in \{0, 1\} uniformly across parameter space, \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} is mis-specified.
2. If no indicator X predicts P_{\mathrm{collapse}} better than chance, the model lacks predictive content.
3. If \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \emptyset for all \beta — i.e., no configuration is viable — the constraint set is over-restrictive and the model has no empirical content.
4. If \chi(\mu) is uniformly smooth for all (\rho, \tau_{\mathrm{norm}}, \sigma) and all \mu grids, H5 is refuted.
5. If the network transition is no sharper than the single-node null model, the phase-transition interpretation of H5 is refuted, even if a transition exists.

