B.2 Theorem 2.2 (HJB characterization)

Statement. Under standard assumptions (f is Lipschitz in (x,u), bounded on compact sets, \sigma uniformly elliptic), the value function

V(t,x) = \sup_{u(\cdot)} \mathbb{P}\!\left[x(s)\in\mathcal{K},\ \forall s\in[t,T]\ \middle|\ x(t)=x\right]

is the smallest nonnegative lower-semicontinuous viscosity supersolution of

-\partial_t V - \sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\mathcal{L}^u V = 0

on (0,T)\times\mathcal{K}, with boundary data V(T,\cdot) = 1_{\mathcal{K}}(\cdot) and V(t,x) = 0 for x\notin\mathcal{K}, where \mathcal{L}^u is the generator of the controlled diffusion.

Proof sketch (via dynamic programming).

Step 1 — DPP. For 0 \le t \le t+h \le T, the strong Markov property gives

V(t,x) = \sup_{u(\cdot)}\mathbb{E}\!\left[ 1_{\{\tau_{\mathcal{K}} > t+h\}}\, V(t+h, X_{t+h}) \ \middle|\ X_t = x\right].

Step 2 — Supersolution property. Applying Itô's formula to V(t+h, X_{t+h}) and using the DPP, one obtains

V(t,x) \ge \sup_{u}\mathbb{E}\!\left[V(t+h, X_{t+h})\right]

which, after division by h and letting h \to 0, yields

-\partial_t V - \sup_u \mathcal{L}^u V \ge 0

in the viscosity sense.

Step 3 — Minimality. Let W be any nonnegative lsc viscosity supersolution with W(T,\cdot) = 1_{\mathcal{K}} and W = 0 outside \mathcal{K}. By the comparison principle for viscosity supersolutions, W \ge V on [0,T]\times\mathcal{K}. This is the classical result of Buckdahn–Quincampoix–Rainer–Rascanu for stochastic viability. Hence V is the smallest such supersolution.

Step 4 — Level set. By definition of \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T},

x_0 \in \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} \iff V(0,x_0) \ge \beta.

Hence \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \{x : V(0,x)\ge\beta\}. \square

Remark. The distinction from the "viscosity solution" formulation is that a viscosity solution of a first-order or second-order equation is unique when the equation is proper, but here the equation has multiple supersolutions. The value function is the smallest supersolution, not merely a solution. This is the correct characterization for the exit-time problem.

