B.3 Theorem 2.3′ (finite-horizon margin theorem)

Statement. Assume:

· f : \mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{W}\to\mathbb{R}^n is Lipschitz in x with constant L_x and in w with constant L_w;
· \mathcal{W} is bounded with \|w\|_\infty \le W_{\max};
· T<\infty;
· x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{det},T} with a deterministic viable trajectory x^*(\cdot) satisfying

\delta_K := \inf_{t\in[0,T]}\operatorname{dist}(x^*(t),\partial\mathcal{K}) > 0.

Then x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} for every

\beta \le \mu_w\!\left(\left\{w : \|w\|_\infty < \frac{\delta_K L_x}{L_w(e^{L_x T} - 1)}\right\}\right).

Proof.

Step 1 — Deviation bound. Fix the control u^*(\cdot) that realizes the deterministic viability of x_0. Define e(t) = x(t;x_0,u^*,w) - x^*(t). Then

\dot{e}(t) = f(x^*(t)+e(t), u^*(t), w(t)) - f(x^*(t), u^*(t), 0).

Using the Lipschitz property,

\|\dot{e}(t)\| \le L_x\|e(t)\| + L_w\|w(t)\|.

By Grönwall's inequality,

\|e(t)\| \le \frac{L_w\|w\|_\infty}{L_x}\big(e^{L_x t} - 1\big) \le \frac{L_w\|w\|_\infty}{L_x}\big(e^{L_x T} - 1\big).

Step 2 — Safe disturbance set. Define

\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{safe}} := \left\{w : \|w\|_\infty < \frac{\delta_K L_x}{L_w(e^{L_x T} - 1)}\right\}.

For w\in\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{safe}}, we have

\|e(t)\| < \delta_K \quad \text{for all } t\in[0,T].

Hence

\operatorname{dist}(x(t),\partial\mathcal{K}) \ge \operatorname{dist}(x^*(t),\partial\mathcal{K}) - \|e(t)\| > \delta_K - \delta_K = 0.

Therefore x(t)\in\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) for all t\in[0,T], i.e., \tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0,u^*,w) > T.

Step 3 — Probability bound. Hence

\mathbb{P}[\tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0,u^*,w) > T] \ge \mu_w(\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{safe}}) \ge \beta

whenever \beta \le \mu_w(\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{safe}}). Taking the supremum over u(\cdot) shows x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}. \square

Corollary B.2. Under the hypotheses of Theorem 2.3′, if \mu_w(\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 1 (i.e., all admissible disturbances are safe), then x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{1,T}.

Remark B.3. The bound is conservative: the true safety radius may be much larger because the Grönwall bound is a worst-case estimate.

---

B.4 Theorem 2.4 (stochastic vs. robust)

Statement. Assume \mathcal{W} is the support of \mu_w, \mathcal{W} is compact, and V is continuous in x uniformly in t. Then

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty}\quad \text{for all } \beta\in[0,1].

Proof. Let x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}. By definition, there exists u^*(\cdot) with

\tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0, u^*, w) = \infty \quad \text{for all } w\in\mathcal{W}.

Since \mathcal{W} = \mathrm{supp}(\mu_w), the set \{w : \tau_{\mathcal{K}} = \infty\} has \mu_w-measure 1. Hence for any T<\infty,

\mathbb{P}[\tau_{\mathcal{K}}(x_0,u^*,w)>T] = 1.

Taking the supremum over u, V(0,x_0) = 1 \ge \beta for all \beta\in[0,1]. Therefore x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty}. \square

Corollary B.4. Under the same hypotheses, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}\subseteq\bigcap_{\beta<1}\mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty}. The converse inclusion fails in general.

Counterexample to the converse. Consider \mathcal{K} = [0,1], f(x,u,w) = -x + w, \mathcal{W} = [-1,1], \mu_w = \mathrm{Unif}[-1,1]. Take x_0 = 1, u\equiv 0. Then x(t) = e^{-t} + \int_0^t e^{-(t-s)}w(s)\,ds. For w\equiv -1, x(t)\to -1, so x_0\notin\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}. However, x_0\in\mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty} for small \beta because w\equiv 0 gives x(t)\to 0\in\mathcal{K}. Hence \mathcal{V}_{\beta,\infty}\not\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rob}}.

