B.7 Proposition 5.5 (LP bounds and slack vanishing)

Statement. If the state C(t)\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}, then the LP of Definition 5.4 satisfies

0 \le D_k(S,t) \le \Delta t \sum_{i\in S} d_{ik},

and \xi_{ik}^* = 0 for all i\in S when M > M_0 := \Delta t \sum_{i\in S} d_{ik}.

Proof.

Step 1 — Upper bound. By (F1), 0 \le z_{ik} \le d_{ik}, so

\sum_{i\in S} z_{ik} \le \sum_{i\in S} d_{ik}.

Multiplying by \Delta t and subtracting M\sum_i \xi_{ik} \ge 0,

\Delta t\sum_i z_{ik} - M\sum_i \xi_{ik} \le \Delta t \sum_i d_{ik}.

The bound follows by taking the supremum.

Step 2 — Feasibility of the zero-slack solution. Since C(t)\in\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}}, we have B_i^k(t) \ge B_{i,\min}^k for all i,k. The candidate r\equiv 0, z\equiv 0, \xi\equiv 0 satisfies (F1), (F2), (F3), (F6) trivially. For (F5),

B_i^k(t) + \Delta t\big(Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) - \beta_k B_i^k(t) - R_{ik}(t)\big) \ge B_{i,\min}^k.

If this holds for all i, then \xi^* = 0 is feasible.

Step 3 — Slack minimization. If the above fails for some i, set

\xi_{ik}^{\min} := \max\!\left(0,\ B_{i,\min}^k - B_i^k(t) - \Delta t\big(Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t) - \beta_k B_i^k(t) - R_{ik}(t)\big)\right).

The LP must have \xi_{ik} \ge \xi_{ik}^{\min}. To show \xi_{ik}^* = \xi_{ik}^{\min}, we argue by penalty dominance. Suppose a solution (\tilde r,\tilde z,\tilde\xi) has \tilde\xi_{ik} > \xi_{ik}^{\min} for some i. Then we can reduce \tilde\xi_{ik} by \delta := \tilde\xi_{ik} - \xi_{ik}^{\min} without violating (F5) (since (F5) is the only constraint involving \xi and it is \ge). This strictly increases the objective by M\delta. Therefore any optimal solution has \xi^* = \xi^{\min}.

*Step 4 — Threshold M_0$.* The trade-off between increasing \(\xi_{ik} and increasing z_{ik} matters only when increasing z_{ik} would require more slack. Increasing z_{ik} by \delta z requires the same increase in the left side of (F5) (which is where the buffer depletion comes from), so it may force \xi_{ik} to increase by \delta z (in units). The benefit is \Delta t\cdot\delta z; the cost is M\cdot\delta z. Choosing M > \Delta t suffices to prioritize slack minimization over consumption. Since \sum_i d_{ik} is the maximum possible z-increase, M_0 = \Delta t\sum_i d_{ik} is a sufficient threshold. \square

