B.13 Proposition 7.1 (binary cascade as step-function limit)

Statement. If g_{k\ell} and \tilde g_{ji} are step functions g(x) = \mathbf{1}_{x<\theta}, then the quantitative update of Definition 7.4 reduces to the binary operator \Phi on the set of nodes/functions whose capacity falls below threshold.

Proof. If g(x) = \mathbf{1}_{x<\theta}, then

g_{k\ell}\!\left(1 - \frac{Q_{ik}}{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}}}\right) = \mathbf{1}_{Q_{ik}/Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}} > 1-\theta}.

This equals 1 (no degradation) iff Q_{ik} > (1-\theta)Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}}, i.e., Q_{ik}/Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}} > 1-\theta. Equivalently, it equals 0 (full degradation) iff the failure indicator (i,k) is active relative to threshold 1-\theta.

The product \eta_{i\ell} = \prod_k\cdots\prod_j\cdots is 0 iff at least one factor is 0, i.e., iff there exists (j,k)\in F with \mathcal{D}_{k\ell} > \theta_D or \mathcal{C}_{ji} > \theta_C (with the corresponding threshold 1-\theta being exceeded). Identifying \theta = 1-\theta_D and \theta = 1-\theta_C recovers the binary operator \Phi. \square

