B.14 Proposition 7.2 (fixed-point convergence on the failure set)

Statement. Let g_{k\ell},\tilde g_{ji} be nonincreasing Lipschitz, and \theta_q\in(0,1). Define \Phi_q:\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{F})\to\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{F}) by

(i,\ell)\in\Phi_q(F) \iff Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod}}(t+\Delta t; F) < \theta_q Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{prod},\mathrm{nom}}.

Then \Phi_q is monotone w.r.t. inclusion, \Phi_q(\emptyset) = \emptyset if the nominal state is non-failing, and for any F_0, the sequence stabilizes in at most |\mathcal{N}|\cdot|\mathcal{F}| iterations.

Proof.

Step 1 — Monotonicity of \eta. For each i,\ell, the cascade multiplier is

\eta_{i\ell}(F) = \prod_{k\ne\ell} g_{k\ell}\!\left(1 - \frac{Q_{ik}(F)}{Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{nom}}}\right) \cdot \prod_{j\ne i}\tilde g_{ji}\!\left(1-\frac{Q_j^{\mathrm{agg}}(F)}{Q_j^{\mathrm{agg},\mathrm{nom}}}\right).

Since g is nonincreasing, g(1 - Q/Q^{\mathrm{nom}}) is nondecreasing in Q. Adding more elements to F decreases Q, hence increases 1 - Q/Q^{\mathrm{nom}}, hence decreases g, hence decreases \eta_{i\ell}. So \eta_{i\ell} is nonincreasing in F.

Step 2 — Monotonicity of Q. From Definition 7.4,

Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F) = \big[Q_{i\ell}(t) + \Delta t(\mu_i(Q^{\mathrm{nom}}-Q_{i\ell})_+ - \gamma_\ell Q_{i\ell})\big]\cdot\eta_{i\ell}(F).

The bracket is fixed (depends only on time t). Since \eta_{i\ell} is nonincreasing in F, Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F) is nonincreasing in F.

Step 3 — Monotonicity of \Phi_q. If F_1\subseteq F_2, then for each i,\ell, Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F_1)\ge Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F_2). Therefore

(i,\ell)\in\Phi_q(F_1) \implies Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F_1) < \theta_q Q^{\mathrm{nom}} \implies Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;F_2) < \theta_q Q^{\mathrm{nom}} \implies (i,\ell)\in\Phi_q(F_2).

So \Phi_q(F_1)\subseteq\Phi_q(F_2).

Step 4 — Base case. If the nominal state has Q_{i\ell}(t) = Q_{i\ell}^{\mathrm{nom}} for all (i,\ell), then \eta_{i\ell}(\emptyset) = 1, so Q_{i\ell}(t+\Delta t;\emptyset) = Q^{\mathrm{nom}} (assuming no decay at nominal). Then \Phi_q(\emptyset) = \emptyset if Q^{\mathrm{nom}} \ge \theta_q Q^{\mathrm{nom}} (which holds for \theta_q \le 1). Otherwise the base case must be adjusted.

Step 5 — Convergence. By Proposition 2.5 (Knaster–Tarski), \Phi_q has a smallest fixed point containing F_0, reached in at most |\mathcal{N}|\cdot|\mathcal{F}| iterations. \square

