B.15 Connectivity indicator \rho_c

Statement. For \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}} a row-normalized coupling matrix with nonnegative entries and diagonal excluded,

\rho_c = 1 - |\lambda_2(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}})| \in [0,1].

Proof.

Step 1 — Eigenvalue location. By Perron–Frobenius, \lambda_1 = 1 is an eigenvalue of \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}} with corresponding nonnegative eigenvector. All other eigenvalues satisfy |\lambda_i|\le 1, since \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}} is stochastic.

Step 2 — Range. Hence |\lambda_2(\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{norm}})| \in [0,1], so \rho_c = 1 - |\lambda_2| \in [0,1].

Step 3 — Interpretation. |\lambda_2|=0 (perfect mixing) gives \rho_c=1; |\lambda_2|=1 (disconnected or periodic) gives \rho_c=0. \square

Remark B.6. The v1.2 indicator \mu_{\mathrm{spec}} = 1 - \lambda_{\max} is identically zero because \lambda_{\max} = 1 for any row-stochastic matrix. The corrected indicator uses the subdominant eigenvalue and is non-degenerate.

