B.16 Signed distance properties

Statement. The function \delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C) of Definition 4.7 is locally Lipschitz on \mathcal{X}\setminus\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} and satisfies \delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C) = 0 iff C\in\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}.

Proof.

Step 1 — Lipschitz. The unsigned distance d(C,\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}) is 1-Lipschitz in C by the triangle inequality:

|d(C_1,\partial\mathcal{V}) - d(C_2,\partial\mathcal{V})| \le d(C_1,C_2).

The sign function \mathrm{sign}(\delta_{\mathcal{V}}) is constant on each connected component of \mathcal{X}\setminus\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}. Hence \delta_{\mathcal{V}} is 1-Lipschitz on each component.

Step 2 — Zero level set. \delta_{\mathcal{V}}(C) = 0 iff d(C,\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}) = 0 iff C\in\overline{\partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T}} = \partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} (closed). \square

Remark B.7. The signed distance is not differentiable on \partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} in general, but its square is C^1 if \partial\mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} is C^2.

