Appendix C — Algorithmic Details and Complexity

C.1 Complexity of the Flow LP

Proposition C.1. Algorithm 1 (flow LP) has polynomial complexity in |S| and |\mathcal{F}|. Specifically:

· Variables: O(|S|^2 |\mathcal{F}|) for r, O(|S||\mathcal{F}|) for z, O(|S||\mathcal{F}|) for \xi.
· Constraints: (F1) O(|S||\mathcal{F}|); (F2) O(|S|^2|\mathcal{F}|); (F3) reduces the effective variable count; (F5) O(|S||\mathcal{F}|); (F6) O(|S||\mathcal{F}|).
· The LP is solvable in time O(N^3 L) where N is the number of variables and L the encoding length, or polynomial via interior point methods.

Proof. The variable count and constraint count follow directly from the definitions. Solvability in polynomial time follows from Karmarkar's algorithm or the ellipsoid method. \square

C.2 Complexity of the Cascade

Proposition C.2. Algorithm 3 (quantitative cascade) runs in O(|\mathcal{N}|^2|\mathcal{F}|^2) per time step.

Proof. For each (i,\ell)\in\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{F} (there are |\mathcal{N}||\mathcal{F}|), we compute the product over k\ne\ell\in\mathcal{F} and j\ne i\in\mathcal{N}, which costs O(|\mathcal{F}| + |\mathcal{N}|). Total: O(|\mathcal{N}||\mathcal{F}|(|\mathcal{F}|+|\mathcal{N}|)) = O(|\mathcal{N}|^2|\mathcal{F}| + |\mathcal{N}||\mathcal{F}|^2). The bound O(|\mathcal{N}|^2|\mathcal{F}|^2) is a loose but valid upper bound. \square

C.3 Complexity of Algorithm 4 (Monte Carlo)

Proposition C.3. Algorithm 4 has cost O(N_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot T/\Delta t\cdot(\mathrm{cost\ of\ LP} + \mathrm{cost\ of\ cascade} + \mathrm{cost\ of\ transition})).

Proof. Direct. The dominant term is the LP, which is polynomial in |S| and |\mathcal{F}|. \square

C.4 Regularization Error (Entropic LP Smoothing)

Proposition C.4. Let L(r,z,\xi) be the LP objective and L_\varepsilon(r,z,\xi) = L(r,z,\xi) - \varepsilon H(r,z,\xi) where H is a strictly concave entropy term. Then:

· The regularized LP has a unique optimum (r_\varepsilon^*,z_\varepsilon^*,\xi_\varepsilon^*).
· The map C\mapsto (r_\varepsilon^*,z_\varepsilon^*,\xi_\varepsilon^*) is Lipschitz in C with constant O(1/\varepsilon).
· The suboptimality satisfies 0 \le L_\varepsilon^* - L^* \le O(\varepsilon \log N).

Proof. Standard results in entropic regularization of LP. Strict concavity of H yields uniqueness; strong convexity yields Lipschitz continuity of the argmax; the duality gap estimate follows from the entropy bound. \square

