Appendix D — LP Duality and Economic Interpretation

D.1 Dual of the Flow LP

The primal flow LP (for fixed k, dropping the penalty term for clarity in the dual) is:

\max_{r,z,\xi} \ \Delta t\sum_{i\in S} z_{ik} - M\sum_{i\in S}\xi_{ik}

subject to:

z_{ik} \le d_{ik} \quad [\alpha_{ik}]

r_{ij}^k \le \Gamma_{ij}^k \quad [\beta_{ij}^k]

B_i^k + \Delta t\Big(Q_{ik}^{\mathrm{prod}} + \sum_j r_{ji}^k - \sum_j r_{ij}^k - z_{ik} - \beta_k B_i^k - R_{ik}\Big) + \xi_{ik} \ge B_{i,\min}^k \quad [\eta_{ik}]

r,z,\xi \ge 0.

The dual variables:

· \alpha_{ik}\ge 0: shadow price of demand d_{ik} — the marginal value of relaxing demand.
· \beta_{ij}^k \ge 0: shadow price of link capacity \Gamma_{ij}^k — congestion price.
· \eta_{ik}\ge 0: shadow price of the buffer constraint — the marginal value of a unit of buffer at node i.

The dual feasibility conditions imply the KKT system:

\Delta t - \alpha_{ik} - \Delta t\eta_{ik} \le 0, \quad z_{ik}(\Delta t - \alpha_{ik} - \Delta t\eta_{ik}) = 0,

-\beta_{ij}^k - \Delta t\eta_{ik} + \Delta t\eta_{jk} \le 0, \quad r_{ij}^k(-\beta_{ij}^k + \Delta t(\eta_{jk} - \eta_{ik})) = 0,

-M + \eta_{ik} \le 0, \quad \xi_{ik}(-M+\eta_{ik}) = 0.

These are the standard complementarity conditions of the flow LP.

D.2 Economic Interpretation

· \eta_{ik} is the marginal value of buffer at node i for function k. It is the "price" the planner would pay to relax the minimum-buffer constraint by one unit.
· At the optimum, r_{ij}^k > 0 implies \eta_{jk} \ge \eta_{ik} (flows from low-price to high-price nodes), with the difference bounded by the capacity price \beta_{ij}^k/\Delta t.
· z_{ik} > 0 implies the marginal value of consumption \alpha_{ik}/\Delta t balances against the buffer price \eta_{ik} and the objective coefficient \Delta t.
· The penalty M acts as a ceiling on \eta_{ik}: once \eta_{ik} \ge M, the slack \xi_{ik} becomes active.

D.3 Max-Flow Min-Cut Interpretation

Proposition D.1. In the special case where buffers are infinite (B_{i,\min}^k = -\infty), regeneration cost is zero, and demands are unbounded, the LP reduces to a maximum flow problem, and its optimum equals the min-cut of the temporal network G(t).

Proof. With infinite buffers and no demand caps, the problem is \max\sum_i z_{ik} subject to flow conservation and capacity. Standard LP duality (the max-flow min-cut theorem of Ford and Fulkerson) applies. \square

Remark D.1. The buffer balance and slack terms generalize the classical max-flow by introducing storage, leakage, and infeasibility penalties. The dual prices interpolate between flow-congestion prices and buffer-stock prices.

