Appendix G — Falsifiability Protocol

G.1 Pre-Registered Falsifiers

The framework is refuted if any of the following holds:

(G1) P_{\mathrm{survival}}\in\{0,1\} uniformly across the parameter grid. Diagnosis: \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{MP}} mis-specified.

(G2) No indicator X\in\{PI_{\mathrm{dist}},\widehat{CM},\rho_c,T_{\mathrm{rec}}\} predicts P_{\mathrm{collapse}} better than chance. Diagnosis: model lacks predictive content.

(G3) \mathcal{V}_{\beta,T} = \emptyset for all \beta\in(0,1]. Diagnosis: constraint set over-restrictive.

(G4) \chi(\mu) is uniformly smooth for all (\rho,\tau_{\mathrm{norm}},\sigma). Diagnosis: H5 refuted.

(G5) The network transition is no sharper than the single-node null model. Diagnosis: phase-transition interpretation of H5 refuted.

G.2 Statistical Power

For a Monte Carlo estimate \widehat P with N_{\mathrm{sim}} samples, the standard error is

\mathrm{SE}(\widehat P) = \sqrt{\frac{P(1-P)}{N_{\mathrm{sim}}}}.

To detect a difference \Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} at 95% confidence with power 1-\gamma, we need

N_{\mathrm{sim}} \ge \frac{2P(1-P)(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\gamma})^2}{\Delta_{\mathrm{MP}}^2}.

For P=0.5, \Delta_{\mathrm{MP}} = 0.1, \alpha = 0.05, \gamma = 0.2:

N_{\mathrm{sim}} \ge \frac{2\cdot 0.25\cdot (1.96+0.84)^2}{0.01} \approx 392.

So N_{\mathrm{sim}} = 10^3 is sufficient for a 0.1 effect at 80% power.

G.3 Level-Set Surrogate Fit

For the surrogate \widehat V, use a Gaussian process with RBF kernel or a neural network with two hidden layers. The empirical margin error is controlled by Proposition B.17. Choose the sample size to achieve |\widehat{CM} - sCM| \le \epsilon for a specified tolerance \epsilon.

