Appendix H — Regularization Details

H.1 Sigmoid Degradation

For the regularized engine, replace step-function degradation with

g^\varepsilon(x) = \sigma\!\left(\frac{\theta - x}{\varepsilon}\right), \quad \sigma(y) = \frac{1}{1+e^{-y}}.

As \varepsilon\to 0^+, g^\varepsilon\to \mathbf{1}_{x<\theta} pointwise on [0,1]\setminus\{\theta\}.

Proposition H.1. With g^\varepsilon as above, the quantitative cascade is Lipschitz in the state with constant O(1/\varepsilon). The binary cascade is recovered in the limit \varepsilon\to 0^+.

Proof. The derivative |(g^\varepsilon)'(x)| = |\sigma'((\theta-x)/\varepsilon)|/\varepsilon \le 1/(4\varepsilon). The cascade product inherits this Lipschitz constant. \square

H.2 Entropic LP Smoothing

The regularized objective is

L_\varepsilon(r,z,\xi) = L(r,z,\xi) - \varepsilon\sum_{i\in S}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{F}}H(z_{ik}) - \varepsilon\sum_{i,j,k}H(r_{ij}^k),

where H(x) = -x\log x for x>0. The regularized LP is strictly concave, has a unique maximizer, and the argmax is Lipschitz in the state (Proposition C.4).

H.3 Regularization vs. Theorem 2.3′

Corollary H.2. With both entropic LP smoothing and sigmoid degradation, the transition map C(t_n)\mapsto C(t_{n+1}) is Lipschitz in C(t_n). Hence the regularized engine satisfies the hypotheses of Theorem 2.3′.

